## Free Games and Apps

Making and distributing a game or app with the software is use
for a permitted purpose, whoever makes it, as long as the game
or app is free of charge.

A game or app is free of charge when:

- nobody has to pay to get it, play it, or use it.  A
  pay-what-you-want price with a minimum of zero is fine;

- nobody pays for content, features, or access in it,
  including in-app purchases, subscriptions, paid add-ons, and
  paid early or exclusive versions;

- it shows no advertising; and

- it is not part of, or a way to promote or sell, a product
  or service that is not free of charge, such as a demo of a
  paid game, a promotional game for a brand, or a companion
  app for a paid service.

None of these stop a game or app being free of charge:

- naming its maker, with links to the maker's website and
  merchandise shop;

- grants to make it, such as from an arts or cultural fund;

- prizes it wins, such as in a game jam; and

- donations under [Donations and
  Patronage](#donations-and-patronage).

## Players and Users

Playing or using a game or app made under [Free Games and
Apps](#free-games-and-apps), as it is distributed, is use for a
permitted purpose, whatever you use it for.  For example, you
may use a free app at work or stream a free game for money.
This does not cover taking the software out of the game or app,
or making other software with it.

## Donations and Patronage

Accepting donations and patronage for your work with the
software is use for a permitted purpose, as long as donors get
nothing in return.  A thank-you, such as a mention in the
credits or a supporter role in a community space, is not
something in return.  Content, features, or early or exclusive
versions of a game or app are.

## Learning Material

Making and selling learning material about the software, such
as tutorials, courses, books, and videos, and earning from it
in other ways, such as from advertising or sponsorship, is use
for a permitted purpose.  This covers the examples and sample
projects made for the material, but not any other game or app.

## Prototypes

Using the software at work, or for your company, to evaluate
it and to make prototypes and proofs of concept is use for a
permitted purpose, until the prototype, or anything made from
it:

- is released to the public;

- is delivered to a client or customer; or

- is used in running your company's business.

Showing a prototype privately, such as to pitch it to a
publisher, client, or investor, does not end this permission.

## Additional Definitions

A **game or app** is any software made with the software.

